---
import Lecture from '../../../layouts/Lecture.astro';
import CodePlayground from '../../../components/CodePlayground.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/Callout.astro';
import Quiz from '../../../components/Quiz.astro';
---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="01-variables-types"
  lessonSlug="03-strings-and-fstring"
  hook="A string is a sequence of characters. f-strings — Python's modern way to weave values into text — are the only string formatting you really need in this course."
>
  <h2>Strings: text in quotes</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>string</strong> is any sequence of characters wrapped in quotes.
    Python accepts either single quotes <code>'...'</code> or double quotes
    <code>"..."</code> — pick one and be consistent. Use the other style when the
    string itself contains a quote.
  </p>

  <CodePlayground
    code={`a = 'hello'
b = "hello"
print(a == b)            # → True. Quote style doesn't change the value.

# When the string contains a quote, use the other style to avoid escaping:
song = "Don't Stop Me Now"
quote = 'He said "ok"'
print(song)
print(quote)`}
  />

  <Callout tone="tip" title="Escape sequences for the awkward cases">
    <p>
      Inside a string, a backslash <code>\\</code> introduces a special character:
      <code>\\n</code> is a newline, <code>\\t</code> is a tab, <code>\\\\</code>
      is a literal backslash, <code>\\"</code> is a quote that doesn't end the
      string.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <CodePlayground
    code={`print("line one\\nline two")
print("col1\\tcol2\\tcol3")
print("She said \\"hi\\"")`}
  />

  <h2>+ glues strings, * repeats them</h2>
  <p>
    The same <code>+</code> you use for numbers also <strong>concatenates</strong>
    strings — it joins them end-to-end. The <code>*</code> operator <strong>repeats</strong>
    a string. Both operands of <code>+</code> must be strings, though.
  </p>

  <CodePlayground
    code={`first = "Ada"
last  = "Lovelace"
print(first + " " + last)        # → Ada Lovelace
print("=" * 30)                  # → ==============================
print("ha" * 4)                  # → hahahaha

# This one fails. Read the error:
# print("Hi " + 5)`}
  />

  <Callout tone="pitfall" title="You can't add a string and a number">
    <p>
      <code>"Hi " + 5</code> raises <code>TypeError: can only concatenate str
      (not "int") to str</code>. You'd have to convert one side:
      <code>"Hi " + str(5)</code> works. But honestly — that's exactly the
      problem f-strings were invented to solve. Keep reading.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>f-strings: the right way to build text</h2>
  <p>
    An <strong>f-string</strong> is a string literal with the letter <code>f</code>
    in front. Inside it, anything in <code>{`{}`}</code> is treated as a Python
    expression and replaced with its value at runtime. This is by far the most
    common way to format text in modern Python.
  </p>

  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    code={`name = "Alice"
age = 18

# The wrong way (works, but verbose and easy to mess up):
print("Hello, " + name + "! You are " + str(age) + " years old.")

# The right way:
print(f"Hello, {name}! You are {age} years old.")`}
  />

  <p>
    Inside the braces you can put any expression — not just a single variable:
  </p>

  <CodePlayground
    code={`price = 4.50
qty   = 3
print(f"Subtotal: \${price * qty:.2f}")        # arithmetic + 2-decimal format
print(f"Average per item: \${price * qty / qty:.2f}")

name = "ada"
print(f"Title-cased: {name.title()}")         # method calls work too
print(f"Length: {len(name)} characters")`}
  />

  <Callout tone="tip" title="The :.2f format spec">
    <p>
      After a colon inside <code>{`{}`}</code>, you can write a format spec.
      <code>:.2f</code> means "float, 2 decimal places". <code>:,</code> adds
      thousands separators. <code>:&gt;10</code> right-aligns in a 10-char column.
      You don't need to memorize these — just know they exist for when you need
      them.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>The three formatting styles you may see</h2>
  <p>
    You'll find old code that uses <code>%</code>-formatting or
    <code>.format()</code>. They still work, but for this course, write f-strings.
  </p>

  <CodePlayground
    caption="All three lines produce the same output"
    code={`name = "Alice"
age = 18

print("Hello, %s! You are %d years old." % (name, age))           # old: %-formatting
print("Hello, {}! You are {} years old.".format(name, age))       # 2010-era: .format()
print(f"Hello, {name}! You are {age} years old.")                 # modern: f-string ← use this`}
  />

  <Quiz
    question='What does this print?  greeting = f"Hi {name.upper()}"  print(greeting)  — given name = "ada"'
    choices={[
      { text: '"Hi ada"',         explain: "Almost — but `name.upper()` is called, not just `name`." },
      { text: '"Hi ADA"', correct: true, explain: "Right. Inside `{}` you can run any expression, including method calls like .upper()." },
      { text: '"Hi name.upper()"', explain: "The whole point of an f-string is that the braces get *evaluated*, not printed literally." },
      { text: 'SyntaxError',      explain: "f-strings allow method calls inside braces — totally legal." },
    ]}
  />

  <Quiz
    question='Which of these correctly puts the value of `qty` into the message?'
    choices={[
      { text: 'msg = "You ordered " + qty + " items"',  explain: "TypeError — can't add a str and an int. Use str(qty) or an f-string." },
      { text: 'msg = "You ordered {qty} items"',         explain: "No `f` in front, so the braces are just literal text." },
      { text: 'msg = f"You ordered {qty} items"', correct: true, explain: "Yes — the `f` activates the braces as expressions." },
      { text: 'msg = "You ordered $qty items"',          explain: "That's Bash / shell syntax, not Python." },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Now you try</h2>
  <p>
    The exercise file <code>03-strings-and-fstring.py</code> in WolfCode asks
    you to write a function <code>build_greeting(name, age)</code> that returns
    a greeting in the f-string style. The tests check both names and ages, so
    your function has to work for any input — don't hardcode <code>"Alice"</code>.
  </p>
</Lecture>
